pouch boundary-line patch 0049 - SIGTERM is the `term` note (signal chunk S-2).

0007 sent SIGTERM as `interrupt`, the note SIGINT owns, because the kernel
had no other catchable terminating note. A child killed with
kill(pid, SIGTERM) therefore died of `interrupt` and its parent read
WTERMSIG == SIGINT, and the dispatcher had to guess which of the two
handlers a delivered `interrupt` was for. The kernel now has `term`
(NOTE_BIT_TERM = 7, default terminate, death recorded as SIGTERM;
operator vote 2026-10-13, docs/ERRORS.md "The terminate-request note").
Two files:

  src/internal/_pouch_signal.h  POUCH_NOTE_BIT_TERM (1u << 7), and the
      supported mask 0x2f -> 0xaf, so __block_all_sigs and
      __block_app_sigs block it too.
  src/signal/_pouch_signal.c    __pouch_sig_to_note maps SIGTERM to
      "term" (kill() and raise() post it); the sigset translations give
      SIGTERM its own bit both ways, so blocking SIGINT no longer blocks
      SIGTERM; the handler dispatches "interrupt" to SIGINT and "term" to
      SIGTERM, and the guess between them is gone. An uncaught "term"
      reaches SYS_NOTED(NDFLT), whose default for it is terminate.

The witness is pouch-hello-signals: a child killed with SIGTERM reads
WTERMSIG == SIGTERM, and a SIGTERM handler runs while SIGINT is ignored.

